      "content": "Here's another revised version with an even stronger focus on teaching language:\n\nTo master the concept of mitosis, students need to understand how this process, which involves the division of a eukaryotic cell into two daughter cells, ensures the genetic continuity of cells in an organism. As they learn about mitosis, students should be able to describe the phases of this process, which include interphase, prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase, and cytokinesis. By understanding these stages and the relationships between them, students will be able to explain how mitosis enables cells to replicate and produce genetic material for growth, repair, and replacement.\n\nBy teaching this fundamental concept of mitosis, students will develop a deeper understanding of cellular biology and the intricacies of inheritance, ultimately building a strong foundation for future learning in the field. I've added more explicit instructional verbs like \"master,\" \"understand,\" \"describe,\" and \"explain\" to emphasize the importance of these language skills for learning biology.",
